<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Praefectus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as prefects of particular personal loyalty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A praefectus, or prefect, was a Roman official appointed to a command or administrative post. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> certain strategically important provinces, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, were governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> of equestrian rank. These appointees were chosen for their personal loyalty to the emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/praefectus.jpg" alt="Praefectus" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssolbergj · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_SPQR_banner.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Praefectus, often with a further qualification, was the formal title of many Roman military and civil officials, from fairly low to high rank. Unlike the elected magistrates, whose authority was vested in their person, a prefect held authority by delegation from a higher authority. Within a prefecture such officials had powers in civil administration, including control of prisons. One early use of the title was religious: in the republican era a praefectus urbi guarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> during the annual sacrifice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feriae-latinae">Feriae Latinae</a> on Mount Alban, which the consuls attended; his former title was custos urbi, guardian of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The praetorian prefect began as the military commander of a general's guard company in the field and grew in importance as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a> became a potential kingmaker under the Empire. From the tetrarchy of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, around 300, the praetorian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> administered the four praetorian prefectures, the level of government above the newly created dioceses and the multiplied provinces. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> itself the praefectus urbi ran the city's administration, the praefectus vigilum commanded the Vigiles, the firemen and police, and the praefectus annonae supervised the grain supply, while other prefects guarded the state treasury and the military treasury.</p><p>Many military commands bore the title. A praefectus alae led a cavalry unit, a praefectus cohortis a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohort</a>, a praefectus classis a fleet and a praefectus castrorum a camp, while the praefectus fabrum was in charge of the trained engineers and artisans called fabri. The praefectus legionis was an equestrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> commander, and the praefectus orae maritimae controlled and defended an important stretch of coast. Some titles named the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">auxiliary troops</a>, such as the Praefectus Laetorum for Germanic troops, notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and the Praefectus Sarmatarum gentilium for troops from the steppes, notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Roman provinces were usually ruled by high-ranking officials, but less important ones were entrusted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a>, military men who would otherwise govern only parts of larger provinces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontius-pilate">Pontius Pilate</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> in this way when it was administered as an annex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Egypt, a special imperial domain and a rich, strategic granary, had a head styled uniquely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustal-prefect">Praefectus Augustalis</a>, governing in the personal name of the emperor; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> introduced the same system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> after conquering it. After the mid-1st century, as a result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana">Pax Romana</a>, governorships passed gradually from military prefects to civilian fiscal officials called procurators, with Egypt the exception.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Praefectus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
